A||||21||Ferrari_cdn||RWD in snow||||1||
Z||000000||Ferrari_cdn||09-08-2001||04:43 PM||||After reading FWD and RWD comparsion thread, I've never really driven a RWD, and my 2m4 would be my first.  I wanted to ask what should I be careful of and if I did happen to understeer or oversteer how can I take control back?  I know I will practice in a parking lot, enjoying myself but I would like to know.  I know it's gonna be hard to get control back once the back end breaks loose.<P>I also need to get a heater block for my car so in our damn cold winters I can plug in the car.  These -30c or under temperatures aren't so forgiving.  <IMG SRC="http://forums.newdoom.com/UBB/smilies/cwm31.gif"> .  Do you know how much those things are and is it hard to install?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Mach10||09-08-2001||04:57 PM||||Ferrari: As weird as it sounds, point the nose in the desired direction, and plant the gas. I'd take it into the parking lot. The RWD will only get you in trouble if you're fooling around. The Fiero isn't all that squirrley. Try my dad's Alfa in the snow    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>Don't go into corners "hot" That's asking for trouble...<BR>If you understeer, drop the throttle ever so slightly. That'll bring things back in line. Drop too hard, and it'll swing your ass out.<BR>For oversteer, point the nose in the desired direction, and give it some more gas. But remember, you'll only really be understeering if you're going too fast around a corner.<BR>With the slightly less torquey 4 cyl, you won't have as much fun learning as I will... I get all    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif">   just thinking about it.<BR>Personally, I just plan on taking it easy this winter.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>As for the block heater, you SHOULD already have one. If not, they are pretty cheap, and easy to install. I'm leaving my car plugged in all the time. I hate freezing my balls off waiting for the car to warm up.<P><BR>EDIT: I remember your previous PM. Your heater core is plugged/leaking, right? PM me, and I'll see if I can source you a decent used one. Do you have A/C or not?<p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 09-08-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000002||Jncomutt||09-08-2001||05:13 PM||||Wait, I'm curious, and confused for that matter.  What is this 'block heater' thing??  I don't think I have one....||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ferrari_cdn||09-08-2001||05:24 PM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B><BR>Personally, I just plan on taking it easy this winter.       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  Sure.  I'll believe that when I see it.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">     I know that sometimes I like playing in the snow.  (providing I'm in an open place and there's no other cars around.  I'm not gonna play on Portage and Main.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">)<P>I WILL get a block heater.  And no, I don't think I said that about the leak.  I don't have A/C.  (damn!)<P>Jncomutt:  A block heater is a thingy that keeps your vital engine parts warm, I don't know the technical stuff but it makes your car car easy to start up in damn cold weathers.  You plug in your car to a wall outlet, as if you're recharging a electrical car    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Ferrari_cdn (edited 09-08-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000004||Mach10||09-08-2001||05:35 PM||||Hey! enough out of you  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>I thought you had mentioned that your heater core had died. Did you mean the thing that warms your interior, or the thing that warms your engine?<P>The block heater is a heating element placed in the coolant system to prevent everything from freezing. It keeps things warm so the oil isn't thick, and the coolant hasn't turned to gel. Makes the engine easier to crank, and makes sure that it gets oil to the right places more quickly (thinner oil flows faster). You know if you have one if you have a power cord dangling in your trunk/engine compartment.||||reg||7
Z||000005||theogre||09-08-2001||08:26 PM||||There are all kinds of heaters.<P>The ones that go in the coolant lines probably won't do you any good in Fiero. <P>There may not be ones that go in the freese plugs for these motors. Those are usually made for big trucks. <P>You can get magnet heaters. you can stick that right on the oil pan.<P>They make(d) one that went down the dipstick hole. Those always seemed kind of pointless to me. not enough surface area to really keep up in cold weather.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mach10||09-08-2001||09:00 PM||||Why not just use the stock one!?!||||reg||5
Z||000007||PBJ||09-08-2001||09:33 PM||||Fiero works great in the snow.  excellent traction for accelerating and good weight balance for braking.  Only concern is ground clearance, I think the city should pay me for snow plowing.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forsalestart.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ferrari_cdn||09-09-2001||03:39 AM||||I just said that my car does not have a block heater.  I just need to install one.||||reg||1
Z||000009||My7Fieros||09-09-2001||03:50 AM||||Snow!? Block heaters?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> What the hell is a block heater? I have to find ways to keep my Fiero running <B>cool</B>! Man how cold does it have to get for you to have to buy something that keeps and <B>engine</B> warm!?<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B><I>My 7 Fieros</FONT></FONT></B></I>||||reg||11
Z||000010||LS1swap||09-09-2001||07:45 AM||||GM did have a block heater for the fiero according to the fiero online service guide it is option code KO5 don't know if what engine that is for maybe the 4 & 6 cylinders both have the same size freeze plugs. I would think it was special order for anything other than the ones shipped to Canada. A  well tuned car with a good battery will start easily down to -20 F . My diesel pick up has one and I have to admit it is nice to go out and  start it on those -20 F mornings and have instant heat. Personally I  don't drive my fiero in the winter. The salt they put down will tear them up. As far as driving it in snow it is just common sense take it easy and the skinnier snow tires the better they dig down through to the road surface better than wide ones .Also if they let you use studded snow tires in Canada that would be the way to go||||reg||1
Z||000011||Ferrari_cdn||09-09-2001||03:55 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by My7Fieros:<BR><B>Snow!? Block heaters?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> What the hell is a block heater? I have to find ways to keep my Fiero running [b]cool</B>! Man how cold does it have to get for you to have to buy something that keeps and <B>engine</B> warm!?<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> <P><BR>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>LOL<P>Not under freezing weather!  A warm engine starts up faster and easier when warm, and not when its frozen.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>And yes we can use studded wheels, but with a limit time.  I don't know when we can start using it, but up to March, then gotta change back to regular tires.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Mach10||09-09-2001||04:05 PM||||Well, it cost me $23 for the part, and about $20 to install. I'm sure we could do it in an hour or two. The real biatch is to route the power cable in such a way that it doesn't get caught in the pulleys. Not sure about the 4-cyls, but for mine, it's in the WORST possible location.<P>Sorry Ferrari: I thought you were talking about your interior heater core. I remembered that from a previous discussion. I guess you meant the block heater  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000013||Ferrari_cdn||09-09-2001||07:57 PM||||Quite allright.  My car is going to be here any time now.  I'll get the block heater maybe in a few weeks.  We'll just need to set a time  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||fieronv||09-09-2001||08:02 PM||||Ferrari_cdn:<BR> You might also want to invest in a battery blanket. You should be able to get a block heater for both the 2.8l and 2.5l engines. As for running the cables I'm not sure but with plenty of tie wraps you should be able to route it out of the way of everything. <BR><P>------------------<BR>85GT (twoloud)||||reg||3
Z||000015||David Bartlett||09-09-2001||09:04 PM||||Watch out for snow buildup on city streets.. you know the ruts, they will move you around alot.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Ferrari_cdn||09-09-2001||10:12 PM||||Tell me about it David, I know!  I almost crashed into a parked vehicle because the ruts wouldn't let me change lanes!||||reg||11
Z||000017||8Ball||09-09-2001||11:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by My7Fieros:<BR><B>Snow!? Block heaters?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> What the hell is a block heater? I have to find ways to keep my Fiero running [b]cool</B>! Man how cold does it have to get for you to have to buy something that keeps and <B>engine</B> warm!?<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> <P><BR>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>My7 I moved back to Tn after living in Nova Scotia for a while. Believe you me, We have no winters here. Just a really long fall  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I was standing outside during the last lunar eclipse on the east coast watching it. Was -50c and my beer was freezing faster than I could drink it. Dang COLD!!!!!!!!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Brandon86SE||09-09-2001||11:38 PM||||I have to agree.  The worst part about driving a Fiero in the winter is the ground clearance, and the ruts.  I didn't really have many problems last year as far as cornering and such go.  I just drive a heck of a lot slower in the winter.  The only problem I had was hitting a patch of black ice and losing control.  I ended up doing a couple donuts in the middle of one of Calgary's main streets (Glenmore).  Lucky for me it was Sunday morning, and I didn't hit anyone, or the rail.  But black ice can happen to anyone.  Overall, it wasn't too bad.<P>Brandon||||reg||1
Z||000019||GarethWright||09-10-2001||10:29 AM||||Here in OK, we don't usually get Tons of snow in the winters, but the ice and freezing rain can be a real problem.  I live about a mile from my neighborhood entrance, and the city doesnt't plow neighborhood roads, so when we get snow, people drive on it until it compacts down, and freezes solid overnight, Not Fun to drive on.  Fortuneatly for me though, Broken Arrow and Tulsa just use sand on the road, not salt.  But then you have lots of loose sand on the road come spring-time.||||reg||1
Z||000020||84Bill||09-10-2001||12:54 PM||||A fiero in the snow can be best described with two words. WOOOOO HOOOOO! <BR>I have had my 2m4 in snow so deep when I opend the door it plowed snow. Never have been stuck for more than a minute where simple rocking would not get me out. The car handels great in the snow so long as you dont go ape sh!t with speed. <BR>Take it to an empty lot and go have some fun.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000021||William Federle||09-10-2001||07:57 PM||||I have a 1984 SE. About a month after I got it (two yrs ago) someone stole all four wheels. I replaced them with the 13" steel wheels. The reason I mention that is that I heard that with the 14" wheels a Fiero is like a toboggan in the snow. I know that it definitely isn't with the 13" wheels. <P>I drive to Fond du Lac from Milwaukee to work and in the winter I can use cruise unless there is glare-ice. When the rear end is about to let go, you get a warning - it starts to fish tail a little. All you have to do is back off on the gas to regain control.<P>||||reg||1
